 freeman@MasPar.COM  (Jay R. Freeman) writes:
>the same vein, a criterion often used for classifying species into genera
>is that members of different species within the same genus can have
>offspring but those offspring cannot themselves have further offspring.

 Having studied this general subject fairly thoroughly for a taxonomy course
at Yale in the 70's, I haven't heard of this criterion. Either it is new,
or it is only used for vertebrates. The standard classifications for plants
and insects definitely do not work this way. (There is little standardization
on the meaning of any taxon other than "species").

 anton@hydra.unm.edu (Stanton McCandlish) writes:
>Quoth Perry E. Metzger, verily I saith unto thee:
