>(as far as we can tell) from Felis domesticus, yet they can be successfully
>bred.  If I am wrong, and this definition does still hold without serious
>challenge, I stand to be corrected.

 The standard definition is that members of a species do interbreed under
natural circumstances, not that they can be made to interbreed. This is
a slippery distinction under some conditions.

>of the time.  I'd also remind you that there really may be no such thing
>as a species; "species" is just a human categorization imposed on what we
>observe.

 Some organisms fit into the concept quite well. With others (such as
asexually reproducing organisms), it is purely an invention for our
convenience.

