Date: Thu, 17 Jun 1993 17:37:10 -0400
From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com>
Subject: SOC/CHAT: Wolf behavior 
X-Reposting-Policy: redistribute only with permission

Stanton McCandlish says:
> Quoth Perry E. Metzger, verily I saith unto thee:
> 
> > Domestic dogs can interbreed freely with wolves, and produce fertile
> > offspring, which is the usual definition of "species". 
> 
> That is a rather old and limiting definition.  I had heard this was
> suspect.  Ever heard of a lyger?

Not fertile. Neither are mules.

> Asiatic wildcats are a separate species
> (as far as we can tell) from Felis domesticus, yet they can be successfully
> bred.  If I am wrong, and this definition does still hold without serious
> challenge, I stand to be corrected.

To my knowledge, its still the usual definition, although the
definition is sometimes weakened (I doubt you can get Great Danes and
Chihuahuas (sp?) to breed). I might be wrong, but as I said, all
indications are that they are really the same species.

> > Indeed,
> > domestic dogs now appear, from genetic studies, to be nothing more
> > than domesticated wolves that have been selectively bred for five
> > thousand years.
> 
> Hate to ask for a ref,

Well, there was the episode of "Nature" on dogs (or was it on both
dogs and cats) about five years back (when I had a TV set). They noted
that dogs actually bear the strongest genetic similarity to the
smaller asiatic wolves rather than the larger western hemisphere
variety, which are also pretty much the same species, by the way. I've
also seen some articles in places like Science Times. I never paid
them too much attention, since it wasn't a subject near and dear to my
heart, but I'm willing to bet that a literature search proves me right.

> Last I heard, dogs were a mix of wolves and other things,
> more or less "dogs", that were NOT wolves.

This I've never heard.

> Native Mexicans also had some
> sort of dog, long long before contact with anyone from the E. hemisphere
> (unless you buy the Prince Llewellyn's Voyage idea!), and it seems HIGHLY 
> unlikely that 2 isolated populations would selectively breed wolves into
> very similar dogs.

Ask yourself this: where did the Native Mexicans come from? Also note
that there is evidence of humans having kept dogs around back to the
ice ages.

