> than something with some noticeable (and often annoying) "zing".

Significantly increased intelligence *is* a noticeable effect.  If
the increase is only marginal, it's probably not worth the money, to
me at least.  Long-term memory is harder to test this way, but I'll
wring all I can out of mnemonic tricks before moving on to
vasopressin.  Lacking the capability to subject myself to the
appropriate psychological tests, I have to settle for
self-observation.  As I can tell when I'm dumbed down for one reason
or another, I can probably make a decent guess as to whether I'm
doing better instead.

The relevant effect of amphetamine and related substances (which
does not include caffeine) is probably not their stimulant action
per se -- the whole euphoria-insomnia-jitters complex -- though this
must come into play for all-nighters.  Their effects on attention
span and focus are probably responsible for the significant
enhancement of intellectual performance found in double-blind tests
(though double-blindness is pretty much moot on the subject's side
here).  This effect explains the use of amphetamine-complex drugs in
attention-deficit disorder.  There are in fact drugs developed for
this condition which attempt to separate this from the stimulant
component.  They'd probably not be approved for "enhancement" use,
though they seem less abusable than classical stimulants.

> For example caffeine can make me drum my fingers like crazy, making
> me _look_ alert, but the brain can still feel sleepy/low energy.

I get exactly this off of caffeine, which is why I don't use it.


