ChrisM wrote:

> [...] Since it would almost seem that, under this definition, if I bring a
> raccoon into my house* where it can't interbreed with the wild ones "under
> natural circumstances" then I have created a new species!  (the "housecoon?")

But if you move a population of raccoons to an isolated area -- perhaps an
island -- where they cannot interbreed regularly with the population from
which they were taken, and keep them isolated for lots and lots of
generations, then random genetic drift and natural selection may well have
allowed speciation to take place -- something may have happened to the
animals in one population or the other so that they are no longer capable
of interbreeding.

That's not to say it will happen for sure; only that reproductive isolation
provides an opportunity for speciation.

